        "problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{3}\u2061x+{tan}^{2}\u2061x{cos}^{3}\u2061x)\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}\u2061x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{3}\u2061x+{tan}^{2}\u2061x{cos}^{3}\u2061x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{3}\u2061x(1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{3}\u2061x{sec}^{2}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{3}\u2061x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}\u20610 \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
